The first corpus fault, found by a seventh implementation

The first byte of a part request is the hashmap exhausted flag, and
what the reference does with it is compare it:

    RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED
        wants_more_hashmap = True if request_data[0] == ... else False

cmd/dump and tools/gen both read the byte as a boolean instead, so
every non-zero value said exhausted, and the flag decides whether four
bytes of last map hash sit between it and the resource hash. One vector
turned on the difference, because tools/gen had chosen 0x01 for the
flag when it built the plaintext:

    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted-short
        plaintext        01aaaaaaaa
        minimum_length   37          the mapped threshold, from 0x01

Reticulum-Go compares the byte against 0xff, as the reference does,
answered 33, and was right. The comparison is now in both programs, the
plaintext is 0xff and the vector was regenerated; its raw changed
because the plaintext did, and the pinned randomness gave it the same
initialisation vector. No other vector carries a flag byte that is
neither 0x00 nor 0xff.

This is the first defect a foreign implementation has found in the
corpus rather than the other way round, and it is the argument for
harnesses in one sentence: an implementation that disagrees with a
vector is evidence about the vector too.


The second corpus fault, found by a vector that had no implementation to
disagree with

A resource advertisement is a msgpack map of eleven one-letter keys, and
the reference reads it by key:

    RNS/Resource.py#unpack
        adv.t = dictionary["t"]

It names all eleven and it names them in an order pack does not write
them in, which settles what the format allows: neither the order of the
map nor its size is a rule of it. doc/resource had said so since it was
written. cmd/dump had not: it walked the keys in the order pack writes
them and gave up on the first that did not match, so a map the reference
reads correctly would have printed eleven dashes.

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-reordered
        the same eleven keys and values, in the order unpack reads
        them; the same length, and not the same bytes

cmd/dump now locates each key and prints in the corpus's own order, and
a key that is not there costs its own field and no other. No existing
vector changed.

The vector passes in all three implementations that read an
advertisement at all, so it is a finding about nothing but the corpus.
That is the difference from the fault above: no implementation
disagreed, because the ones that could have were right. A coverage
build disagreed instead, and the argument it makes is the other half of
the one above: a decoder written twice is worth the second writing, and
neither writing is the authority. The reference is.

The third corpus fault, found the way the second was

The reference reads an advertisement by looking eleven names up in a
dictionary umsgpack built for it, and a name it has no field for is a
name it never asks about:

    RNS/Resource.py#unpack
        adv.t = dictionary["t"]

So a twelfth key costs nothing. cmd/dump read the map itself and
required every key to be a one-byte string, which is every key any
advertisement the reference writes today holds, and it gave up on the
first that was not. A map with one extra pair in it cost ten of the
eleven fields behind that pair.

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-extra-key
        a twelfth key, named in two bytes and holding a float64,
        second in the map; the eleven still read

cmd/dump now steps over a key of any shape the reference could have
written, sharing the body of mp_skip to do it. All three
implementations that read an advertisement at all pass the vector, so
like the fault above it is a finding about nothing but the corpus, and
about the one direction a decoder is asked to get right before there is
anything to get right: what it does with a key upstream has not added
yet.


The fourth corpus fault, found by measuring

This one is the other way round again, and it is the first found by a
run rather than by a coverage build or a foreign implementation.

A vector was written for a plaintext that stops inside a length prefix,
the byte that says how long the resource hash is. Its expect claimed
the three keys ahead of the cut, on the corpus's own rule for a msgpack
body that runs out: the reader stops and what it read stands.

That rule is the rule for an array. It is not the rule for a map, and
the reference says so:

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#InsufficientDataException

umsgpack raises while it unpacks, unpack is handed no dictionary, and
not one of the eleven names reads. The two implementations here that
read an advertisement at all both printed eleven dashes and both were
right; the corpus was wrong, and it was wrong in the direction that
matters, claiming that a decoder may report fields out of a map the
reference refuses.

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-truncated
        eleven dashes, and three whole keys ahead of the cut that
        are not among them

Both readers now refuse the map, and the same rule settles the vector
beside it: a map that names one key twice is no map either, which is
finding 35. The array rule is untouched and every array vector still
holds it.

What found it is worth the entry. The vector had been through cmd/check
and through a coverage build, and both agreed with it, because both
were reading a rule this repository had written down rather than the
reference. Two independent implementations disagreeing is what a
conformance run is for, and this is the second time it has been the
corpus that was wrong.


The fifth corpus fault, in the same reader as the second and the third

Both of those were cmd/dump reading an advertisement in a way the
reference does not, and this is the third. umsgpack unpacks a map
before RNS/Resource.py#unpack looks a name up in it, so a value with no
field here is a value the reader must measure and step over, whatever
its type. cmd/dump knew eight of them, which is every type an
advertisement the reference writes holds, and gave up on any other: a
negative number, a boolean, a signed width, a string too long for
fixstr, an array or a map, and the whole map went to dashes.

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-foreign-types
        five pairs of no field here, holding every type umsgpack can
        write into a packet, and the eleven behind them

The map header itself was the second half of it. Eleven keys with five
pairs beside them is 16 pairs, which is where msgpack moves the count
out of the head byte, and cmd/dump read the low four bits of a map16 as
a pair count of five.

Both are now read as the reference reads them, and tools/gen grew the
same widths, because its own reading of raw had exactly the same eight
types in it. That is the second reader written twice agreeing twice on
the same mistake, and doc/resource had not said this one either way.

Two of the three implementations that read an advertisement pass the
vector; the third is finding 40.


The sixth corpus fault, in the same reader again, one run later

The fifth said cmd/dump knew eight msgpack widths and gave up on any
other, and taught it the rest. The rest turned out to be five short.
Three widths were left out on a written reason, and each reason was
about what would be inconvenient to ask the reference for rather than
about what the reference can write:

    ext, six widths     "umsgpack writes them only for a handler
                        someone registered". It writes one for any
                        umsgpack.Ext a caller packs, and nothing is
                        registered here or in the reference.

    float32             "written only for a caller that forces single
                        precision". force_float_precision is an
                        argument of packb. Passing it replaces no
                        function and every byte stays umsgpack's.

    the 256-byte class  bin16 and str16 were held and never taken;
                        ext16 was not held at all. The reason on file
                        was that such a value does not fit beside the
                        types foreign-types carries, which is true and
                        is not the reason it was read as.

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext-types
    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext16
    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-float32
        one pair of no field each, and the eleven behind it

An ext value is where a decoder can be wrong in a way the bin widths
never let it be: the length counts the body and the type byte sits
outside it, so a reader that measures an ext as a bin lands one byte
inside the pair after it. That is what ext16 asks and what bin16, the
same 256 bytes without the type byte, does not.

tools/gen grew the same three widths for the same reason, which is the
second reader written twice agreeing twice on the same gap, a run after
the first time it happened. What kept it from being a third is that
doc/resource carried the three reasons in writing where they could be
read again.


Six of the seven produce the adopted signature from the private key and
the message, bit for bit, through their own signer. The seventh cannot
load an identity from a private key at all; see finding 7.

All seven agree on both forms of a delivery proof to a destination
where the proof names the packet it proves correctly, and two of them
verify the signature over the wrong one of the two hashes when it does
not; see finding 28. All seven agree on both delivery proofs made on a
link once the harness hands them the key that signs one. Which key that is, they do not
decide: only Reticulum-Go, go-reticulum and rsReticulum have a link
proof path at all, and the other four verify no proof on a link
anywhere. See doc/link.

The channel envelope is read by two implementations of the seven.
Reticulum-Go has Channel.HandleInbound and go-reticulum Envelope.Unpack,
and both read all six bytes; rsReticulum has Envelope::unpack and reads
four of them, discarding the declared length exactly as the reference
does, so it cannot reproduce the two bytes it did not keep. The other
four define the context byte and have no code behind it.


What a result covers

Not every field came from the implementation. Six of the seven expose
no entry point that yields the parsed announce fields; their validator
returns only a boolean. For those the harness follows the
implementation's own code step by step, using its constants and its
primitives, and marks the place. Such fields test the transcription as
much as the implementation.

    implementation    derivations  announce fields  link id  signalling
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    rsReticulum       called       called           called   called
    microReticulum    called       transcribed      called   called
    Reticulum-Go      called       transcribed      called   transcribed
    go-reticulum      called       transcribed      called   transcribed
    Reticulum-rs      called       transcribed      called   absent
    rns.js            called       transcribed      called   absent
    sgiath/reticulum  called       transcribed      called   absent

Three words, three different things. Called means the harness handed
the bytes to the implementation and printed what came back.
Transcribed means the implementation has the code and does not export
it, so the harness follows it line by line and says where. Absent means
the implementation has no such code at all, and the harness prints a
dash rather than a value borrowed from the reference. A dash fails the
vector, which is the point: see finding 14.

Derivations are the identity hash, the name hash, the destination hash,
Ed25519 verification, and for the encrypted and link vectors the key
derivation and the decryption. Verdicts are signature_valid,
hmac_valid, the plaintext, and where the implementation offers one, its
own accept or reject. All findings below rest on called code or on
source, not on transcription.

Every one of the seven derives the link id through its own code. Six
have a function for it; sgiath/reticulum has none, and its packet hash
is called instead, which is the same derivation minus the step that
belongs to links. See finding 9.

The link request proof is the one place where two implementations have
nothing to call: Reticulum-rs and sgiath/reticulum verify no proof
anywhere. For those the material is assembled as the reference
assembles it and handed to their own Ed25519 verifier, so the curve and
the encoding remain theirs.

Three of the harnesses reach past the implementation for the X25519
agreement alone, because go-reticulum, Reticulum-rs and
sgiath/reticulum keep the private key unexported or expose no exchange.
Each uses its language's standard curve, which is what those
implementations use internally.

All seven agree on the destination hash for names with at least one
aspect, the announce payload layout, the assembly of signed data
including the position of app data, Ed25519 verification, and both
forms of a delivery proof.

Every one of the seven computes the packet hash through its own
function and verifies the proof signature with its own verifier.
Reticulum-Go and go-reticulum both have a proof validator with
exported fields, so their signature_valid is that validator's own
accept or reject over the whole proof rather than a bare signature
check.

Five of the seven decrypt a packet from the reference correctly. The
encrypted vectors turned up one implementation that cannot decrypt any
of them, one that agrees on the shared secret and can derive nothing
from it because the salt is an identity hash it never computed, and one
whose ratchets do not work, all for reasons the announce vectors could
not have shown.

One of the six carries a link from request to established traffic
without a single divergence. Two cannot establish one with the
reference at all, a third establishes one and then decrypts nothing on
it, and a fourth establishes one without ever reading what was
signalled.

Reticulum-Go is a second. It establishes the link, derives the same
keys, decrypts every packet on it, and reads more of what those packets
carry than any of the six. Six of its nine link failures are thresholds
on a payload too short to be what its context byte said it was:
findings 22, 23, 26 and 27. Not one of those is a packet it reads
wrongly. The other three are, and all three are a msgpack value it
reads or refuses differently: findings 32, 35 and 39.


