    proof/link-id-mismatch         passed by all seven

The three proofs are the entry that says something good, and they were
written because the corpus could not say it. Every proof vector agreed
with itself: signature_valid and both fields that say where a proof
arrived had only ever been printed as yes for that kind, so a decoder
computing none of the three would have passed all seven. All seven
implementations compute all three, and each of the new vectors breaks
one claim while leaving the other two standing.

Of the four advertisement vectors, the two that turn on refusing a map
are the ones with a finding behind them, and the extra key is the one
the three readers all got right. That is the second time a vector
written to find a defect found it in cmd/dump instead, and the third
and fourth entries above say what each of them was.

Four took it from 131 to 135. Three are packets to a destination and
one is a link data packet, and all four are of kinds every harness
runs.

    encrypted/empty-token          failed by six, findings 36, 37
                                   and 41
    encrypted/long-padding         failed by five, findings 36 and 38
    encrypted/loose-padding        failed by five, findings 36 and 39
    linkdata/resource-advertisement-foreign-types
                                   failed by five, finding 40

The three tokens are the first vectors here to ask an implementation
for two verdicts about the same packet: that the hmac is right and that
the token behind it does not open. Nobody had had to answer that
before, because pad writes no padding either bound refuses, and it is
the first place where a decrypt call that returns a plaintext or an
error is not enough. Two implementations answer with one verdict for
both and print the wrong one, which is finding 36; a third answered
that way because its harness did, and that is the seventh harness
fault. Two more fail all three for the reasons they already fail every
encrypted vector, findings 5 and 7.

Three of them are stricter than the reference in the same two places
and for the same reason, and one says so in a comment about padding
oracles. That is finding 39, and it is the only finding in the corpus
where the implementations are defensible and the reference is not: the
reference's own unpad removes three bytes on the word of one of them.

The fourth is the advertisement and it costs Reticulum-Go a failure
where it had been the strongest of the seven. The two that pass it use
a msgpack library and step over a value they have no field for, which
is what the format requires and what cmd/dump had to be taught. See the
fifth corpus fault above.

One of the three tokens ends its run in a core dump, and that is
finding 37. It is the first vector in the corpus to crash an
implementation rather than to disagree with one, and what it takes is
48 bytes: an iv, an hmac, and no ciphertext for the padding length to
be read from.

Five took it from 135 to 140, all of them link data packets and all of
them the same shape: an advertisement with one pair of no field, whose
value is a width no vector had carried.

    linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext-types
                                   failed by five, finding 40
    linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext16
                                   failed by five, finding 40
    linkdata/resource-advertisement-float32
                                   failed by four
    linkdata/resource-advertisement-bin16
                                   failed by four
    linkdata/resource-advertisement-str16
                                   failed by four

Four of the seven fail all five for the reason they already fail almost
every advertisement, and the numbers say so: rns.js and sgiath fail all
16 of them, microReticulum 15 and Reticulum-rs 13. Nothing new is
learned from those four here.

The three that read advertisements are where the five earn their place,
and they split. rsReticulum and go-reticulum pass all five, which is the
same answer they gave to foreign-types and for the same reason: a
msgpack library measures a value it has no field for and the eleven
names still resolve. Reticulum-Go passes the float and both 256-byte
values and fails the two that hold an ext, which narrows finding 40 from
"a value it has no width for" to a family it has no case for. Its map
reader has the widths; it does not have the type byte.

The three widths cmd/dump had to be taught are the sixth corpus fault,
and it is the second time this reader has been taught the same lesson.


Finding 1: the hop limit is not applied at parse time

Affects six of the seven implementations tested. Vector
test/announce/hop-limit.

python-rns rejects a packet whose hop count is at or above
PATHFINDER_M, that is 128, inside Packet.unpack, before any other
field is examined (RNS/Packet.py#PATHFINDER_M). Six of the seven
implementations do not. All six decode the packet fully and report it
as a valid announce.

Where a limit exists at all, it sits further in and reads differently:

    Reticulum-Go      packet.go:219       hops >= 128, inside Unpack
    go-reticulum      transport.go:4400   hops >= 128 + 1
    rsReticulum       ingress.rs:372      hops >= 128 - 1, announces only
    sgiath            transport.ex:36     routing_max_hops, source only
    rns.js            none found
    Reticulum-rs      none found
    microReticulum    none checked at parse

go-reticulum's threshold is one higher than the reference. A packet at
exactly 128 hops is dropped by python-rns and forwarded by
go-reticulum.

Reticulum-Go is the one that puts the check where the reference puts
it, and it checks the same boundary; its COMPATIBILITY.md dates the
change to python-rns 1.3.8 and names Packet.Unpack as the place. It
passes both vectors of the pair.

test/announce/hop-limit-below, the other side of the boundary, passes
in all seven. The pair was added to separate a threshold one hop too
high from one a hop too low from none at all, and against the first six
it separated nothing: none of them applied any threshold at the parse
step, so all six accepted 127 and all six accepted 128. The seventh is
what the pair was for, and it lands on the right side of both. It is
doc/packet, section Hops.


Finding 2: ratchet announces are rejected

Affects Reticulum-rs. Vectors test/announce/ratchet and test/announce/ratchet-and-app-data.

DestinationAnnounce::validate (destination.rs:115) never reads the
context flag and never parses a ratchet. The 32 ratchet bytes are read
as the first half of the signature, so every announce carrying a
ratchet fails signature verification and is dropped.

This is the trap described in doc/announce, section Ratchet. It is the
most consequential finding here: ratchets are enabled per destination
in current python-rns, and an affected node silently loses those
announces rather than reporting an error.


Finding 3: an app name with no aspects gets a trailing dot

Affects Reticulum-rs and microReticulum. Vector test/destination/app-name-only.

The reference joins app name and aspects with a dot between each pair,
so a destination with no aspects hashes the app name alone. Both
implementations append a separator unconditionally:

    Reticulum-rs      destination.rs:70    app_name || "." || aspects
    microReticulum    Destination.cpp:180  if (aspects != nullptr)

microReticulum's guard tests for a null pointer, but the empty-aspect
call path passes "" rather than nullptr (Destination.cpp:164), so the
dot is appended.

    reference       example_utilities     8f28004423519cc633ca
    both            example_utilities.    ada8ea63426e6118c006

Every destination whose name is an app name alone is unreachable
between these implementations and the reference.


Finding 4: non-canonical X25519 scalars

Affects rns.js and microReticulum. Vectors test/keyset/unclamped and test/keyset/clamped.

The reference stores the X25519 private scalar exactly as supplied and
clamps only when the scalar is used, so an unclamped scalar
round-trips unchanged and yields the same public key as its clamped
form. See doc/identity.

    rns.js            rejects the key outright. @noble/curves clamps
                      first and then requires the result to be below
                      2^255 - 19, which excludes two of the 2^251
                      reachable clamped values.

    microReticulum    accepts the key and derives a different public
                      key, and therefore a different identity hash. It
                      does not clamp before the scalar multiplication.

rns.js's case is an edge case: all five upstream keys are already
clamped, and both python-rns backends clamp when generating an identity
key, so a stored identity never reaches that path.

microReticulum's is not, and finding 6 below shows why. It fails
silently with a wrong public key rather than refusing the input, and
ratchet keys are not clamped when generated.


Finding 5: rns.js derives half the key material

Affects rns.js. Eight of the ten test/encrypted/ vectors: every one
that reaches a key at all.

    src/identity.js:237     Cryptography.hkdf(32, sharedKey, this.hash)
    RNS/Identity.py#DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH
                                           length=Identity.DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH,
                                           that is 64

A 64-byte derived key selects AES-256-CBC and splits into two 32-byte
halves; a 32-byte key selects AES-128-CBC and splits into two 16-byte
halves. rns.js takes the second path throughout, down to
src/fernet.js:76, which names aes-128-cbc outright.

The HKDF output agrees as far as it goes: rns.js's signing key is the
first half of the reference's signing key. Only the length differs.

    reference   signing 61d1de0d...639e6aff  encryption a00a15a6...3bdcd28c
    rns.js      signing 61d1de0d73097c427032bac52d5376b0
                encryption 46b9856a53d1e8841cc6f8c9639e6aff

The consequence is total for single-destination traffic: rns.js cannot
decrypt a packet from python-rns 1.4.2, and python-rns cannot decrypt
one from rns.js. The HMAC fails before decryption is even attempted.

python-rns keeps DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH_LEGACY = 32 at
RNS/Identity.py#DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH_LEGACY,
so 32 was once right. This is version skew rather than a misreading,
and it is what a corpus pinned to a version is for.


Finding 6: microReticulum does not clamp, and that breaks ratchets

Affects microReticulum. Vector test/encrypted/ratchet, and the same
root cause as finding 4.

Finding 4 recorded microReticulum deriving a different public key from
a non-canonical X25519 scalar, and called it an edge case because every
key the reference generates is already clamped. Ratchet keys are not.
RNS/Identity.py#_generate_ratchet generates one as 32 raw random
bytes, and a random
scalar is already in clamped form only once in 32 tries.

    ratchet private     267c9c1b...00d48784   as generated, non-canonical
    clamped form        207c9c1b...00d48744
    reference public    c82c57db...1a52fe32   from the clamped scalar
    microReticulum      fa7c42ed...           from the raw scalar

The shared secret differs, the HMAC fails, and the packet is dropped.
microReticulum decrypts the three identity-key vectors correctly, so
the defect is invisible until a ratchet is used.


Finding 7: sgiath/reticulum cannot load an identity from a private key

Affects sgiath/reticulum. 17 of its failures: every
test/keyset/, every test/encrypted/ but the one rejection it refuses on
a length, and test/sign/. One of the 17 is
encrypted/low-order-ephemeral, which an earlier run charged to
finding 16; the implementation refuses that point correctly and fails
the vector here instead.

    lib/reticulum/identity.ex:54    enc_pub = :crypto.generate_key(:eddh, :x25519, enc_sec)
    lib/reticulum/identity.ex:55    sig_pub = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, sig_sec)

Erlang's :crypto.generate_key/3 returns the pair {PublicKey,
PrivateKey}. from_private_key/1 stores that whole tuple where a public
key belongs. update_hash/1 guards on is_binary/1, so it falls through
to its no-op clause and the identity hash stays nil:

    enc_pub  {<<66, 25, 217, ...>>, <<248, 149, 63, ...>>}
    hash     nil

The two generating functions beside it do destructure the pair
correctly (identity.ex:98, identity.ex:104), and from_public_key/1 is
correct. So an identity created fresh works, and an identity loaded
from a stored private key is silently unusable: no public key, no
identity hash, and therefore no destination hash and no HKDF salt.
That is the normal path on every start after the first.

Nothing in test/ exercises from_private_key/1.

This is the finding the corpus was most obviously for. It needs no
network, no peer and no packet: load a private key, state the public
key. A vector asks exactly that.

test/sign shows how far the defect reaches. sig_sec is stored correctly
and the signer would work, but the harness cannot get as far as calling
it: printing the public key comes first and raises on the tuple. An
implementation that cannot state its own public key cannot announce,
whatever its Ed25519 does.

test/encrypted shows how narrow the defect is, which is only visible
since the harness stopped raising at the first field it touches. The
X25519 agreement is right: shared_key matches the reference on every
encrypted vector, ratchet and all. What fails is the line after it. The
salt of the derivation is the identity hash, that hash is nil, and
HKDF raises on it, so every key, every verdict and every plaintext is
absent. One field of the identity carries the whole of it.


Finding 8: microReticulum does not build with GCC 16

src/microReticulum/Utilities/Memory.h uses uint8_t and uint32_t without
including <cstdint>, which older libstdc++ headers supplied
transitively. Building the library needs -include cstdint. Not a wire
format matter, but it stands between the corpus and anyone who wants to
reproduce the microReticulum row.


Finding 9: the link id is taken over the whole request

Affects rns.js and sgiath/reticulum. Vectors test/linkrequest/signalling
and, through it, every linkproof and linkdata vector.

The link id is a truncated hash of the request packet with the
signalling bytes chopped off the end
(RNS/Link.py#link_id_from_lr_packet). Both
implementations hash the packet as it arrived.

    rns.js            src/packet.js:253, getHashablePart, marked
                      "fixme implement properly"
    sgiath            lib/reticulum/packet.ex:92, hashable_part, which
                      has no link caller at all

    reference         4be2a2dc5ff0321315222d5deca76937
    both              e584100893b8bf948c26fd1986254ec7

python-rns always signals an MTU, so the request that opens a real link
is always the 67-byte one, and the id is always wrong. Nothing after
that can work: the proof is addressed to a link id neither recognises,
and the key derivation takes the link id as its salt, so every key on
the link is wrong too.

Both derive the right link id from test/linkrequest/no-signalling, the
form with nothing to trim. That is the shape of the finding: the hash
is right, the trimming is missing. It is also why the corpus carries
the two forms as separate vectors. The vector passes for sgiath and
fails for rns.js, which stumbles one line further on, at finding 10.


Finding 10: rns.js drops every link request the reference sends

Affects rns.js. Vector test/linkrequest/signalling.

    src/link.js:103   if(linkRequestPacket.data.length !== Link.ECPUBSIZE)

One accepted payload length, 64. The reference accepts 64 and 67 and
emits only 67, because Link.__init__ signals unconditionally
(RNS/Link.py#initiator). No link between rns.js and python-rns 1.4.2 is
established in either direction, and the rejection happens before
finding 9 can matter.

rns.js also derives 32 bytes of link key material where the reference
derives 64 (src/link.js:289), which is finding 5 in the link path: even
past the length check, the token would be keyed for AES-128.


Finding 11: microReticulum decrypts one link packet in 32

Affects microReticulum. 29 of the 32 test/linkdata/ vectors. Same root
cause as findings 4 and 6.

    reference       35bb1f6a74b45436fc2dee15496e76511173bd6eb57d36f8...
    microReticulum  f7ac8a90ade7c050b257ce5767232d0ac08df4de1c912f8b...

A link private key is 32 random bytes as generated
(RNS/Link.py#X25519PrivateKey),
and a random scalar is in clamped form only once in 32 tries. python-rns
clamps when the scalar is used; microReticulum does not. The two ends
compute different secrets, the HMAC fails, and nothing decrypts.

Finding 6 recorded this as an edge case that broke ratchets. On a link
it is not an edge case: every link key is drawn the same way, so the
rate is the rate. microReticulum reads the link request correctly and
verifies the proof correctly, and then cannot decrypt a packet on 31
links out of 32.

The corpus now holds the 32nd. Its link sessions are separate, each
with its own responder scalar, and the one behind
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-large came out already in clamped
form:

    resource-advertisement        2c...b5   clamps to 28...75
    resource-advertisement-large  28...65   clamps to itself

microReticulum decrypts the second and fails the first, which is the
diagnosis rather than a counterexample to it. Two more of its passes on
this kind are keepalive and resource-part, the two link packets the
reference does not encrypt. Everything else on a link is the wrong
secret.

That an implementation can pass one vector of a kind and fail 29 on the
value of one byte is the argument for generating each session
separately rather than reusing one link throughout.


Finding 12: rsReticulum accepts a proof stripped of its signalling

Affects rsReticulum. Vector test/linkproof/signalling-removed.

    handshake.rs:229   unpack substitutes mode 1 and MTU 500 when a
                       proof carries no signalling
    handshake.rs:245   validate signs over the substituted bytes

The reference assembles the signed material with no signalling bytes
when the proof has none (RNS/Link.py#signalling_bytes), so a proof with its
signalling cut off fails to verify. rsReticulum puts the defaults back
before verifying, and a proof that signalled exactly those defaults
still verifies with the bytes removed.

Anything on the path can therefore strip three bytes from a proof and
have it accepted. The signalling carries the link MTU, so what is
stripped is a value the two ends were meant to agree on under
signature. The reference notices; rsReticulum does not.


Finding 13: Reticulum-rs accepts any link request of 64 bytes or more

Affects Reticulum-rs. Vector test/linkrequest/invalid-length.

    link.rs:184   if packet.data.len() < PUBLIC_KEY_LENGTH * 2

The reference accepts exactly 64 and 67 and drops everything else
(RNS/Link.py#LINK_MTU_SIZE). Reticulum-rs accepts every length at or
above 64.
Because the excess is chopped before hashing, a 65, 66 and 67-byte
request carrying the same keys all open the same link id.

It reads no signalling either; that is finding 14.


Finding 14: three of seven read no link signalling

Affects Reticulum-rs, rns.js and sgiath/reticulum outright, and
Reticulum-Go and go-reticulum only in that their readers are
unexported. Vectors test/linkrequest/ and test/linkproof/.

The three signalling bytes of a link request carry the mode in the top
three bits and the MTU in the low 21 (RNS/Link.py#signalling_bytes).
Three of the seven implementations have no function anywhere that reads
them:

    rsReticulum       SignallingData::unpack, both directions
    microReticulum    mode_from_lr_packet, mtu_from_lr_packet, and the
                      proof counterparts
    Reticulum-Go      signallingBytes at pkg/link/link.go:3065 and its
                      two readers, all unexported
    go-reticulum      linkModeFromProofPacket at rns/link.go:2798,
                      unexported
    Reticulum-rs      none
    rns.js            none
    sgiath            none

The three with none still form a link, because AES-256 is what they
would have used anyway and 500 bytes is what they assume. What they
cannot do is honour a peer that signals anything else. The MTU is the
one value the two ends are supposed to agree on before any data
flows, and it is signed into the proof, so it is not advisory.

These four vectors were passing until the harnesses were corrected.
Three of them printed the mode the reference would have decoded, which
matched expect and passed, for implementations that decode nothing.
A conformance harness that fills in a field the implementation does
not compute credits it with code it does not have, and that is worse
than a harness that reports too many failures. The rule is now written
down under "What a result covers", and the numbers for Reticulum-rs
and sgiath/reticulum fell by four and one.


Reproducing

    ENCODE=no DUMP=conformance/<impl>/<binary> cmd/check

ENCODE=no because no harness implements the -e direction; it belongs to
the corpus, not to the wire format. See conformance/README for where to
put each implementation and how to build its harness. The diff output
of cmd/check names the exact vector and field behind every claim above.


The proofs, and nothing found

Two of the seven verify no link request proof anywhere (finding 14)
and one of them cannot load an identity from a private key at all
(finding 7), so the delivery proof looked like the place to find a
third divergence. There is none. All seven read both forms, compute the
same packet hash, address the proof to the same 16 bytes, and reach the
same verdict.

That is worth stating rather than leaving as an absence. sgiath/
reticulum fails half this corpus and gets proofs exactly right, because
a proof needs only a public key and a hash: no private key to load, no
link to derive, no ratchet to clamp. Its failures are concentrated
where state is, not where the wire format is.

The two forms are the same signature. What differs is 32 bytes the
receiver already computed, and no implementation trusts them in place
of its own hash.


The interface access codes, measured once

test/ifac was skipped by all six harnesses until the seventh. Skipped
is not a pass and not a failure: it means nobody has looked.

Six of the seven have IFAC code and none of the six exposes it outside
interface plumbing, which needs a socket, a configuration and a running
instance. A harness could follow the code instead, as it does for the
announce fields, but then all six cells would be transcription: six
copies of one reading, which agree by construction and measure nothing.
That is why those six cells are still missing rather than filled in.

Reticulum-Go is the exception and it is one of packaging rather than of
protocol. Its pkg/ifac is a package with no interface in it: New takes
a size, a network name and a passphrase, Key returns the 64 bytes the
mask is derived from, and Unmask takes a frame and returns the packet
and a verdict. The harness calls all three.

    ifac/announce          passed    a network name alone
    ifac/passphrase        passed    both, the two hashes in order
    ifac/passphrase-only   passed    a passphrase alone
    ifac/minimum-size      passed    a one-byte code
    ifac/altered-frame     failed    a code that covers nothing

Four of five, on the first implementation ever asked, and every one of
the four is a frame whose code is correct. The key derivation, the
salt, the order of the two hashes in the origin, the per-byte mask and
the two header bytes that are masked while the code between them is
not: all of it agrees with the reference byte for byte. One value in
the vector is still followed rather than called, the 64-byte origin
itself, which New builds and keeps no copy of. The fifth is what
happens after a code does not match, and is finding 29.

A frame with bit 7 set is not a packet, and an implementation that
reads one as a packet gets random flags, a random hop count and a
destination hash that matches nothing. That is what the kind is for,
and six of the seven remain unmeasured against it.


Finding 15: a 63-byte proof payload is read as an implicit proof

Affects every implementation tested. Vector test/proof/invalid-length.

python-rns reads 64 bytes as an implicit proof and 96 as an explicit
one and drops every other length (RNS/Packet.py#EXPL_LENGTH). None
of the seven checks the length. All take the 63 bytes as a signature,
verify it, and report a decoded proof that does not verify, where the
reference reports nothing at all.

The distinction matters where a proof arrives truncated: the reference
leaves the packet unproved and the receipt open, and these seven mark
it proved-and-invalid, which is a different state.

Reticulum-Go comes closest without arriving. Its PacketReceipt
compares the payload length against both forms
(pkg/packet/receipt.go:165) and returns false for anything else, so it
refuses the proof; what it does not do is refuse the packet, and the
harness prints the fields its decoder still produced. The verdict is
right and the rejection is not reported as one.


Finding 16: the low-order shared secret is returned rather than refused

Affects rsReticulum, Reticulum-rs and microReticulum. Vector
test/encrypted/low-order-ephemeral.

X25519 against a point of small order yields an all-zero secret that
neither end contributed to. The pinned backend raises; these three
return the zero secret, derive a key from it, and report hmac_valid no
rather than "-".

Three refuse it, which agrees with the pin: go-reticulum through
crypto/ecdh, rns.js through @noble/curves, and sgiath/reticulum through
Erlang's :crypto.compute_key. The first two pass the vector. The third
refuses the point and then cannot state the identity hash, which is
finding 7 and not this one.

The count in the previous run was four rather than three, and it was
wrong: two of the three refusals reached the diff as exceptions, which
is the harness fault above, and an exception is indistinguishable from
a wrong answer in a diff. A harness that turns a correct refusal into
a failure manufactures a finding, which is the same defect as one that
fills in a field the implementation never computed, in the other
direction.

Every reader of an announce can compute the derivation that follows,
because the salt is the recipient's identity hash and that is public.
See doc/encryption, section Contributory behaviour.


Finding 17: microReticulum accepts a non-canonical Ed25519 scalar

Affects microReticulum. Vectors test/signature/non-canonical-s and
test/announce/non-canonical-s.

RFC 8032 section 5.1.7 admits only S < L. A signature with S re-encoded
as S+L is a second encoding of one signature; the pinned backend
refuses it and microReticulum's verifier reduces S instead of
range-checking it, so it reports the announce as valid where the
reference drops it.

This is the same split python-rns has between its two backends, which
is why the backend is part of the pin. See doc/identity, section
Canonical signatures.


Finding 18: four implementations name the wrong numbers for a short header

Affects rns.js, rsReticulum, microReticulum and sgiath/reticulum.
Vectors test/announce/short-header, test/announce/short-header-2 and
test/announce/no-header.

18 bytes is one short of a header 1 header and 34 one short of a header
2 header, and nothing about either packet can be read, not even the
context byte. rns.js reports short-payload with a payload length of 0
against a minimum of 148 for both: it reads a header out of bytes that
do not hold one and then measures what is left. The rejection names the
wrong rule, which is the reason the corpus records the numbers behind a
rejection rather than a single word. Nothing in it refuses a frame for
being short at any point: a slice past the end of a Uint8Array is short
rather than an error, so its harness prints "-" where the other six
print a number.

One byte is the other threshold, and it is the only one that is not a
header length: how long a header has to be follows from the header type
bit, which is in the byte that is missing. rsReticulum, microReticulum
and sgiath/reticulum answer 19, the header 1 length, for a packet of
one byte:

    rsReticulum       header.rs:48      raw.len() < HEADER_MINSIZE
    microReticulum    Packet.cpp:426    size() < HEADER_MINSIZE
    sgiath/reticulum  packet.ex:64      a pattern that cannot match

It is the same defect at the other end of the same rule. All three
branch correctly once two bytes are there, which is why all three pass
short-header and short-header-2 and only no-header separates them from
the reference: none of them tells "too short to say which header this
is" from "too short for a header 1".

Two of the seven answer 2 and pass, and neither holds the number:

    go-reticulum      packet.go:433     p.Raw[1] before any test
    Reticulum-rs      serde.rs:48       a second read_byte that fails

Both die on the second byte before the header type is reached, so 2 is
the offset each of them indexed. The seventh answers 3, by a constant
of its own rather than by a misread, which is finding 25.


Finding 19: four of seven read nothing a request or a response carries

Affects Reticulum-rs, microReticulum, rns.js and sgiath/reticulum.
Vectors test/linkdata/request, request-no-data, request-empty-data,
response and response-empty-data. All four fail all five: whether a
body is nil or empty is a distinction below the level any of them
reaches, and the pair added since the last run cost each of them two.

Two further vectors of the group hold a body that gives less than the
context wants, request-truncated and request-not-an-array, and
Reticulum-rs passes both. Its request fields are three dashes on every
request, and three dashes is exactly what those two expect. They are
the only vectors in the corpus where reading nothing is the right
answer, and a reminder that a pass is agreement on an output and not
evidence of a reader. The other three of this group fail both, and so
does go-reticulum, which does read the body; that one is finding 30.

Contexts 0x09 and 0x0a are where msgpack becomes part of the wire
format. Three implementations read them and four do not, and the four
are not alike in how far they get:

    Reticulum-rs      names both contexts, reticulum-core/src/packet.rs:115
    microReticulum    names both, Type.h:426, and has a msgpack decoder
                      in Resource.cpp that nothing points at a request
    rns.js            has both constants commented out at
                      src/packet.js:28, and msgpackr as a dependency
    sgiath/reticulum  has neither constant and no msgpack anywhere

Naming the context is not reading it. All four decode the packet, the
token and the plaintext correctly and then stop, so a request reaches
an application as an opaque blob it has to unpack itself, if it knows
to.

go-reticulum reads both through its vendored umsgpack
(rns/link.go:1543, :1562). rsReticulum has Link::parse_request and
Link::parse_response_plaintext as standalone functions, which is the
easiest request path of the seven to point a harness at. Reticulum-Go
reads both in handleRequest and handleResponse, through a msgpack of
its own that ships as a separate module, and bounds the decoded array
by its element count before touching it.


Finding 20: rsReticulum delivers a msgpack nil as the byte 0xc0

Affects rsReticulum. Vector test/linkdata/request-no-data.

Link.request called with only a path sends a three-element array whose
third element is nil. rsReticulum's parse_request routes every element
through msgpack_value_to_bytes (crates/rns-link/src/link.rs:97), which
returns the bytes of a binary or a string and otherwise re-encodes the
value:

    } else {
        let mut encoded = Vec::new();
        rmpv::encode::write_value(&mut encoded, value)?;
        Ok(encoded)
    }

For nil that is one byte, 0xc0, so an application asking for a request
body gets one byte of msgpack framing where the reference gives it
nothing. A request with no data is the ordinary case: it is what
Link.request produces when called with a path alone.

The same function is on the response path, so a response with no body
carries the same byte.


Finding 21: a resource part is read as a token by nobody, and one
implementation of seven reads every context that names one

Affects six of the seven implementations tested. Vectors
test/linkdata/resource-*.

Seven contexts carry a resource transfer and the implementations read
between one and all of them:

    context           Q4Go  go    rsRet  micro  rs-rs  rns.js  sgiath
    -----------------------------------------------------------------
    0x02 adv          own   own   own    own    -      -       -
    0x03 req          own   -     -      -      -      -       -
    0x04 hmu          own   -     own    -      -      -       -
    0x06/0x07 cancel  own   -     -      -      -      -       -

"own" is the implementation's own decoder; a dash is code that does not
exist. Q4Go is Reticulum-Go, which has all four. Where its decoder is
exported the harness calls it, and where it sits inside a method that
wants a live transfer the harness follows it and says so at the line.
For the other six, three can read an advertisement, one can read a
hashmap update, and none has a parser for a part request or for either
cancel; theirs are read inline in a transfer loop and yield nothing an
observer can call, which is a different thing from being absent, and it
is why those harnesses print a dash rather than following the code. A
transfer loop is not a decoder. Reticulum-Go's are functions that take
a plaintext, decide from it, and bound every read by its length, which
is why they are followed instead.

The part request is the one worth having. Its first byte decides where
the resource hash begins, and an implementation that assumes a fixed
offset is right until the receiver runs out of hashes. See doc/resource.
Reticulum-Go is the only one that reads that byte at all, and it is
also the one that reads it the way the reference does, against 0xff
rather than against zero; the corpus had that wrong and the entry above
on the corpus fault says so.

There are three advertisements since this run. The second carries
metadata, which is bit 5 of the flags byte, and the third is a resource
of 100000 bytes, whose sizes, part count and hashmap need msgpack
encodings no other vector reaches. Three of the four that read an
advertisement pass all three: not one of them reads the flags byte as a
constant or the integer widths as fixed. The fourth is microReticulum,
which reads an advertisement correctly and decrypts only one of the
three; see finding 11. The three that read no advertisement fail all
three.

microReticulum reads an advertisement correctly and still fails the
vector, because it decrypts nothing on a link at all; see finding 11.
rns.js has all seven constants commented out at src/packet.js:20, so it
does not name the contexts either and fails the part on the context
byte alone.

Nothing here is measured for the resource proof: it is a kind of its
own and all seven exit 77 for it. No implementation of the seven has a
resource proof path to point a harness at.


Finding 22: six of seven implementations bound no link plaintext by its
length, and the seventh bounds every one at the wrong number

Affects all seven, in two different ways. Six vectors, one for each way
a payload can be shorter than the context byte that named it promises:

    vector                          corpus  Reticulum-Go  the other six
    ------------------------------------------------------------------
    resource-request-empty               1            33  no rule
    resource-request-short              33            33  no rule
    resource-request-exhausted-short    37            33  no rule
    resource-hashmap-update-short       33            32  no rule
    channel-short                        6             6  no rule
    request-short                        1     no rule    no rule

The first four were added on 2026-08-06 and the last two on 2026-08-10,
where the context is not a resource. Every one of the first six
implementations fails all six vectors, and no passing count moved when
either pair was added. Three of them, go-reticulum, rsReticulum and
Reticulum-rs, decrypt the token correctly and print the plaintext, and
then produce nothing about its length; the other three fail earlier in
the packet and never reach it. What the vectors ask for is a rejection
naming the rule, which none of the six has code for. go-reticulum
comes closest on request-short, where its msgpack reader runs out and
reports "insufficient data" -- an error, but not the rule and not the
threshold.

Reticulum-Go has a length rule everywhere the reference has one except
the msgpack request, and it gets two of the five right. What it does
not do is name them: each is a guard that returns, so a receiver sees a
packet ignored and no reason. The three it gets wrong are findings 25
and 26. The two it gets right are the channel envelope, at
ChannelHeaderSize, and the unmapped part request, at the flag and the
hash. Its guards are also why its harness is not in the list of harness
faults above: it is the only one of the seven whose reads into a link
plaintext were bounded by the implementation rather than by the author.

A part request, a hashmap update and a channel envelope are read by
length and carry no framing that can run out, so the plaintext is the
only bound a reader has. cmd/dump had the same defect until 2026-08-06:
it read requested_hashes as len - 33 bytes, which underflows as an
unsigned length on any request shorter than that, and printed memory
until it died. That is what these vectors are for.

Reaching it requires the link key, which both ends of a link hold and
which this corpus publishes. It is not a remote defect in any of the
seven; it is an unchecked length in a decoder, and where the decoder
runs on a device with no memory protection the difference matters less
than it reads. Reticulum-Go is the counter-example that shows the cost
of the check is a comparison.


Finding 23: a link data payload too short for a token names no rule

Affects all seven. Vector test/linkdata/short-payload, added
2026-08-07.

Finding 22 is a plaintext too short for the context byte that named it.
This is the layer above: a payload of 47 bytes cannot hold an iv and an
hmac, so it is not a token at all, and expect names that rule rather
than decomposing it. Every one of the seven fails the vector, and none
of the seven accepts the packet.

What six of them do is drop it through the hmac and not through a
length. Handed the payload, they derive the link keys, verify an hmac
over bytes that are not a token, get no match, and report no plaintext.
python-rns does the same: Token.verify_hmac raises
only at 32 bytes or fewer
(RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#Cannot) and returns False above that,
so a 47-byte payload is refused by a failing hmac there too. On
behaviour the six and the reference agree.

The seventh has the length rule and puts it 16 bytes too high.
Reticulum-Go's decryptWithKeys refuses anything under 64 bytes, an iv,
a whole block of ciphertext and an hmac, where the reference refuses
under 48 and lets a token with no ciphertext through to the hmac. The
gap is the 16 bytes of one empty block, and no vector in the
corpus lands in it: an empty plaintext still pads to a full block, so
every token the reference produces is at least 64 bytes anyway. It is
recorded because it is a threshold, not because a packet is lost to it.

The disagreement is in what is reported, which is what the corpus
measures, and it is the same sentence as finding 22: the packet is
dropped and nothing says why. Read this row as a reporting difference
and not as an accepted packet.

The vector is worth the eight lines it costs anyway, because of where
it landed first. All six harnesses cut the token into iv, ciphertext
and hmac by hand, none of the six implementations exporting the three,
and all six cuts assumed a payload long enough to hold them:

    go-reticulum      slice out of range, caught by its own recover,
                      which printed one error line for every field
    rsReticulum       panic, slice index starts at 16 but ends at 15
    Reticulum-rs      panic, the same slice
    sgiath/reticulum  binary_part raised, and the rescue did the same
                      as go-reticulum's recover
    microReticulum    size() - 48 wrapped round; printed a 31-byte
                      ciphertext and a 32-byte hmac out of a 47-byte
                      payload, overlapping by 16, both still inside it
    rns.js            slice() clamps rather than throwing; the same
                      overlap

Every one is fixed above, and no count moved: the fixes changed six
crashes and misreadings into six honest dashes, which fail the vector
for the reason the finding names.


Finding 24: a link request mode of zero is read as the default

Affects go-reticulum. Vector test/linkrequest/unnamed-mode.

The mode is the top three bits of the link request's signalling word.
python-rns reads them and returns what it read
(RNS/Link.py#MODE_BYTEMASK). Only MODE_AES256_CBC, which is 1, is
enabled (RNS/Link.py#ENABLED_MODES), and signalling_bytes refuses to
emit any other (RNS/Link.py#ENABLED_MODES), so a request signalling
MODE_AES128_CBC, which is 0, is one the reference reads as 0 and will
not produce.

go-reticulum reads the same three bits and then replaces a zero with
the default:

    link.go:2792    if modeBits == 0 { return linkDefaultMode }

linkDefaultMode is MODE_AES256_CBC. A request that signalled AES-128 is
therefore recorded as having signalled AES-256, and the one value that
would have caused the reference to decline is the one value
go-reticulum cannot see. It is the mode the reference numbers 0 and the
Go port numbers 0 as well; the collision is with the port's own
LinkModeDefault sentinel, which is -1 everywhere except here.

Reticulum-Go, microReticulum and rsReticulum print the byte and pass.
Reticulum-Go's reader is the same three bits and the same mask, at
link.go:3446, with nothing behind it that substitutes a default, so the
two Go ports differ on exactly one line. Reticulum-rs and
sgiath/reticulum read no signalling at all and print "-", which is
finding 14 and not this one. rns.js drops every link request, which is
finding 10.


Finding 25: a two-byte frame is refused before its header type is read

Affects Reticulum-Go. Vector test/announce/no-header.

One byte is the threshold that is not a length of anything: how long a
header has to be follows from the header type bit, and that bit is in
the byte that is missing. The reference reads the flags and the hops
and fails on the next slice, so two bytes is the least a frame can be
before the question can even be asked, and the corpus records that
number.

Reticulum-Go refuses at three:

    pkg/packet/packet.go:219    if len(p.Raw) < MinPacketSize
    pkg/packet/constants.go     MinPacketSize = 3

Nothing is lost to it. A two-byte frame carries a header type and
nothing the type selects, so both the reference and Reticulum-Go drop
it; they name different numbers for why. This is finding 18 with the
threshold one too high instead of 17 too high, and it is the
only vector of the twelve that name a minimum length where Reticulum-Go
names a number the reference does not.


Finding 26: a hashmap update is bounded by the hash and not by the
array behind it

Affects Reticulum-Go. Vector
test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-short.

An update is 32 raw bytes and then a msgpack array of two, so the least
it can be is 33: the hash and one byte of the array. Reticulum-Go stops
one short of that:

    pkg/link/link.go:1791    if len(plaintext) < sha256.Size

A 32-byte update therefore reaches the msgpack decoder with nothing to
decode, which fails, and the update is dropped there instead. The
packet is lost either way and the two disagree on which rule lost it.

The same method reads the two fields correctly once the array is
present, which is the other half of finding 21: this is the only
implementation of the seven that produces segment_index and hashmap for
an update at all.


Finding 27: a part request too short is measured against the threshold
for the other kind of part request

Affects Reticulum-Go. Vectors test/linkdata/resource-request-empty and
test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted-short.

The flag byte decides the length: without it the request is 33 bytes,
with it 37, because four bytes of last map hash sit between the flag
and the resource hash. Reticulum-Go checks the length before it reads
the flag:

    pkg/link/link.go:1492    if len(plaintext) < 1+32          return
    pkg/link/link.go:1499    if plaintext[0] == mapped flag
    pkg/link/link.go:1501        if len(plaintext) < pad+32     return

So a five-byte request that says the hashmap is exhausted is refused at
33 rather than at 37, and an empty payload is refused at 33 where the
reference has not got as far as a threshold: it has no flag to read and
one byte is all it wants. Both are the right verdict at the wrong
number, which is what the corpus records rejections by.

The order is also the safe one. Reading the flag first and the length
second is how a decoder reads four bytes that are not there, and this
one cannot.


Finding 28: an explicit delivery proof is verified against the hash it
carries

Affects Reticulum-Go and go-reticulum. Vector test/proof/hash-mismatch.

An explicit proof is 96 bytes: 32 that name the packet being proved and
64 of signature. The signature is over the hash the receiver computes
from that packet, not over the 32 the proof carries, and the two are
the same in every proof the reference emits. They are not the same in
this one.

Both implementations report hash_match no, which is the comparison
done, and then signature_valid no, which the reference does not: it
verifies over its own hash and gets yes. Both verify over the carried
hash instead.

Nothing is accepted that should be refused, because the mismatch is
caught by the other field and both drop the packet. What is lost is the
distinction: a proof for another packet and a proof with a broken
signature are the same event to these two, and the second is an attack
where the first is a stray.

It took a mismatched explicit proof to see it. Both pass
test/proof/explicit, where the two hashes agree and either order of
operations gives the same answer.


Finding 29: an access code that fails takes the frame with it

Affects Reticulum-Go, the one implementation whose harness runs the
kind. Vector test/ifac/altered-frame.

Unmask at pkg/ifac/ifac.go rebuilds the packet, recomputes the code
over it and compares, and returns nil for the packet when the compare
fails. So the two values the corpus asks for after a failure, the
packet that came out of the mask and the code it should have produced,
do not exist to be printed:

    packet             -
    expected_ifac      -
    ifac_valid         no

The verdict is right and the frame is dropped, which is what the
reference does with it. The finding is that unmasking and validating
are one call: an implementation that wants to log what a rejected frame
claimed to be, or to tell a wrong key from a corrupted frame, has
nothing to look at.

Four of the five ifac vectors pass, and all four are frames whose code
is correct. The fifth is the only one where the two halves of that call
can be told apart.


Finding 30: a msgpack body that gives less than the context wants is an
error rather than a stop

Affects go-reticulum. Vectors test/linkdata/request-truncated,
test/linkdata/request-not-an-array, test/linkdata/response-not-an-array,
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-no-hash,
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-not-a-map and
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-not-a-number.

The plaintext is one byte, 0x93, a msgpack array header announcing
three elements over a body that holds none. The reference reads the
header, reads no element, and returns three of nothing:
umsgpack.unpackb raises nothing the caller sees, and the request fields
are absent rather than wrong. doc/link, section Request and response,
is written on this.

go-reticulum replaces the whole record with one line:

    error              insufficient data

Everything already decoded goes with it: the header, the link id, the
token it decrypted and verified. The packet is a valid link data packet
whose payload happens to say less than it announced, and an
implementation that answers with one error line cannot report the part
it read.

The second vector is the other way a body can give less: its plaintext
is 0x01, an integer where an array header belongs, so the read never
begins rather than running out. go-reticulum answers the same one line
for both. The reference answers three dashes for both, and the two are
different branches in every reader that has them.

The other three that fail these vectors fail test/linkdata/request as
well and do not reach the body at all.

Four vectors added since carry the same defect into four more shapes,
and its own words name each one:

    response-not-an-array     umsgpack: expected slice/array but got
                              int64
    advertisement-not-a-map   umsgpack: expected map but got int64
    advertisement-no-hash     missing msgpack key "h"
    advertisement-not-a-number
                              unexpected msgpack int type string for
                              key "t"

The third is the one to read twice. A key that is not there is not a
malformed body: everything before it and everything after it is
well-formed msgpack that the reference reads, and only one of eleven
fields has nothing behind it. go-reticulum answers for all eleven, and
for the twenty-five other fields of the record, with one line.

An error line where a field belongs is the harness reporting honestly
what the implementation returned. Nothing in these six vectors is a
packet the implementation reads wrongly. The whole finding is what it
does with a payload it cannot read to the end, and the corpus format is
built so that the difference shows: the reference stops reading, and
this stops reporting.


Finding 31: a map that is short one key loses all eleven

Affects rsReticulum. Vectors
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-no-hash and
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-not-a-number.

The reference reads the eleven keys of an advertisement by name, so a
key that is not there costs the field it would have filled and costs
nothing else: the other ten are in the map, in front of it and behind
it, and reading them needs nothing from the one that is missing.
RNS/Resource.py#unpack, and doc/resource.

rsReticulum prints eleven dashes. It reads the map into a structure
that has to be complete, so the first key that is not where it belongs
ends the record, and a decoder that hands its caller nothing at all
cannot say what it did read. The same happens when a key is there and
its value is of another type: a transfer size written as the string
"1856" costs the ten fields after it as well as itself.

Nothing is at stake for a peer that is the reference, which writes all
eleven every time. What is at stake is a peer that is not, and a
version that adds a twelfth key: the format has no rule against one, the
reference would ignore it, and an implementation that requires the map
it expects will refuse a message the reference accepts.

cmd/dump had the same defect for a different reason and it is the second
corpus fault above. That two decoders arrived at it separately is what
the vector is for.


Finding 32: a string where a number belongs is read as zero

Affects Reticulum-Go. Vector
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-not-a-number.

The transfer size of an advertisement is a msgpack uint. The vector
holds the string "1856" in its place, which is a type the reference
would raise on rather than a number it would read.

    reference     nothing; the field has no value to report
    corpus        transfer_size      -
    Reticulum-Go  transfer_size      0

Zero is worse than a dash and worse than an error. A transfer size of
zero is a value a resource can carry, so nothing downstream of the
reader can tell it from a resource that really is empty, and the ten
other fields of the map are read correctly and printed beside it. It is
the only field of the record that is wrong, which is exactly what makes
it hard to notice.

Reticulum-Go is the implementation that reads an advertisement most
completely, and this is the one vector of the seven added with it that
no implementation got right. The other two that read advertisements
refuse the whole map, findings 30 and 31, and refusing is not right
either. Nobody prints the dash.


Finding 33: a fixed-width hash is lost with the msgpack behind it

Affects go-reticulum and rsReticulum. Vector
test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-not-an-array.

A hashmap update is 32 bytes of resource hash and then a msgpack array,
and the corpus carries one whose array header is the byte 0x01 instead.
The array yields nothing and prints two dashes, which is the rule a
request body follows and doc/resource states for this payload too.

The hash in front of it is not msgpack. It is fixed width, it is
complete, and it is read before the array is reached. Both
implementations print a dash for it:

    resource_hash      -
    segment_index      -
    hashmap            -

They read the payload as one value rather than as two halves, so the
half that cannot be read takes the half that can. Reticulum-Go prints
the hash and passes the vector, which is what makes this a finding
rather than a property of the format: the payload says which resource
the update is for, and that is the field a receiver needs first.


Finding 34: an identify proof of the wrong length is refused and not
reported

Affects all seven implementations, and four of them only in what they
report. Vector test/linkdata/identify-short.

The reference tests the decrypted plaintext of a link identify against
one number before it reads either field out of it, and a proof of any
other length names nobody:

    RNS/Link.py#LINKIDENTIFY
        len(plaintext) == KEYSIZE//8 + SIGLENGTH//8

The corpus carries 127 bytes where 128 are required: the public key is
whole and where a reader would look for it, and the signature is one
byte short. expect names the rule and the length it wants.

All four implementations that establish a link at all pass both valid
identify vectors, read the key, and verify the signature over the link
id. All four fail this one, and none of the four reports an identity
for it:

    Reticulum-Go      four dashes where the identity fields go
    rsReticulum       the identity fields absent
    go-reticulum      the identity fields absent
    Reticulum-rs      the identity fields absent

So no implementation here takes a short proof for a valid one, and what
the vector measures is narrower than its result: the harnesses do not
say that the length was the reason. That is the same shape as the six
harness faults recorded above and it is not yet fixed, because saying
so means reading each implementation's identify path to learn whether
it checked the length or merely failed to verify a truncated signature.
Those are two different implementations and the corpus asks which one.

The other three fail the vector for the reasons findings 7, 10 and 11
already name: they do not decrypt the link packet that carries it.


Finding 35: one name on two pairs is resolved rather than refused

Affects Reticulum-Go and go-reticulum. Vector
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-duplicate-key.

umsgpack refuses a map that names one key twice rather than choosing
between the two values, so the reference is handed no dictionary and no
field of the advertisement reads:

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#DuplicateKeyException
        encountered duplicate key

The corpus carries the reference's own advertisement with the name of
its second key altered to the name of its first. Eleven pairs, eleven
values, ten names, all of them whole.

Reticulum-Go keeps the second value and reports ten fields that are not
in the packet, shifted by one pair:

    transfer_size      1800        the value of the key named d
    data_size          0

go-reticulum's msgpack refuses it exactly as the reference does, and
says so in a line the corpus has no field for:

    error              encountered duplicate key: t (string)

That half of the finding is a harness fault of the kind already
recorded: a refusal is a result, and the fields it gates print "-".
rsReticulum refuses the map and prints the eleven dashes, which is the
answer. Reticulum-rs prints them too and reads no advertisement at all,
so its pass says nothing about it; finding 11 is the same shape one
kind over.


Finding 36: a valid hmac is reported as invalid when the token behind it
does not open

Affects go-reticulum and rsReticulum. Vectors
test/encrypted/empty-token, test/encrypted/long-padding and
test/encrypted/loose-padding.

The reference reaches two verdicts about a token and reaches them in
order. verify_hmac decides the first, and only then is the ciphertext
decrypted and unpadded:

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#decrypt
        if self.verify_hmac(token):
            ...
            return PKCS7.unpad(...)

Every token vector before these three answered both the same way, so
one verdict stood in for two. These three separate them: the hmac is
right and the padding behind it is not something unpad returns a
plaintext for. All three implementations print

    hmac_valid         no

where the corpus holds yes, and none of the three is wrong about the
bytes. What each of them has is one call:

    go-reticulum        token.go:145  Decrypt, and verifyHMAC beside
                        it, unexported
    rsReticulum         rns-crypto/src/token.rs  encrypt and decrypt
                        are the whole public surface, and
                        encryption.rs:21 says why: "Errors are
                        deliberately opaque - padding and HMAC failures
                        are indistinguishable to callers so timing side
                        channels cannot leak which check failed"

rsReticulum's comment is the finding stated by its author, and it is a
defensible design: a caller that can tell a padding failure from an
hmac failure is a padding oracle. It is also why its harness has
nothing to print but the one verdict it got.

Two implementations are the counter-example, and both are a harness
away from these. Reticulum-Go exports cryptography.ValidateHMAC and its
harness has always called it beside Decrypt; Reticulum-rs exports
Fernet::verify and its harness did not, which is the seventh harness
fault above.

What is open here is what the corpus should ask of the other two. A
harness may transcribe an unexported check, as the table under "What a
result covers" records for other fields, and neither of these two
harnesses does for this one. Until that is decided the vectors count
against the implementations, and the reason they fail is written here
rather than in the count.


Finding 37: a token with no ciphertext ends the run in a core dump

Affects microReticulum. Vector test/encrypted/empty-token.

A token of 48 bytes is an iv and an hmac with nothing between them. The
hmac verifies, the ciphertext is empty, and the padding length is read
from the last byte of a block that is not there:

    Cryptography/PKCS7.h:67
        size_t padlen = (size_t)data.data()[data.size()-1];

With size() zero that is data()[-1]. The reference does the same thing
in Python and raises IndexError, which Identity.decrypt catches and
turns into a dropped packet:

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#unpad
        n = data[-1]

microReticulum reads outside the buffer instead. The harness exits 139
and cmd/check reports the vector as a failure with the signal beside
it. It is the only vector in the corpus that ends a run rather than
disagreeing with one, and the packet that does it is 80 bytes on the
wire, needs no key material the sender does not already have, and is
refused by nothing before the read.


Finding 38: a padding length above the block size is applied rather than
refused

Affects microReticulum. Vector test/encrypted/long-padding.

unpad applies one bound and one only, and both the reference and
microReticulum name it:

    Cryptography/PKCS7.h:69
        if (padlen > bs) { throw ... }

The throw is there and the packet still comes back as a plaintext of
length zero rather than as no plaintext:

    plaintext_length   0        where the corpus holds -

Identity::decrypt returns Bytes and has no second channel, so the
exception is caught somewhere behind it and an empty buffer is what
reaches the caller. An empty buffer is also what a token of an empty
plaintext yields, and test/encrypted/empty is that token, so the two
cases are one value. The implementation passes that vector and fails
this one on the same return.

The distance between them is what the corpus is measuring: a receiver
that cannot tell "the packet carried nothing" from "the packet did not
open" delivers the first when it was handed the second.


Finding 39: three implementations check padding the reference does not

Affects Reticulum-Go, rsReticulum and Reticulum-rs. Vector
test/encrypted/loose-padding, and test/group/zero-padding for as long as
it is a skip.

PKCS7.unpad in the reference reads the last byte, refuses it above 16,
and removes that many bytes without looking at any of them:

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#unpad
        n = data[-1]
        if n > bs: raise
        else: return data[:l-n]

All three validate the padding in full, and all three refuse a length
of zero as well. Two wrote the check and the third inherited it:

    Reticulum-Go  pkcs7.go:37   if padding > aes.BlockSize || padding == 0
                  pkcs7.go:45   bad |= plaintext[i] ^ byte(padding)
    rsReticulum   pkcs7.rs:42   if pad_byte == 0 || pad_byte as usize > BLOCK_SIZE
                  pkcs7.rs:51   if b != pad_byte { InconsistentPadding }
    Reticulum-rs  fernet.rs:207 decrypt_padded_b2b_mut::<Pkcs7>, from
                  the aes crate, which validates every padding byte

The vector is a block whose last byte says three and whose two bytes
above it say 0xaa and 0xbb. The reference removes three and delivers
the 13 in front of them; all three deliver nothing.

This is the one finding here where the implementations have the better
of the argument. Unvalidated padding is a padding oracle in the general
case, two of the three authors wrote the check on purpose, and the
third got it from a library that had already decided. It is still a
divergence, and it is one a sender can reach: a packet python-rns
delivers is a packet these three drop.

The second half of it is not measured at all. All three would refuse
test/group/zero-padding, which is a padding length of nought the
reference accepts and removes nothing for, and group is a kind all
seven harnesses skip. The vector that would show it exists and no run
reads it.


Finding 40: a msgpack value with no field costs the whole map

Affects Reticulum-Go. Vectors
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-foreign-types,
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext-types and
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-ext16.

The reference hands the plaintext to umsgpack, which builds a
dictionary out of whatever types it holds, and looks eleven names up in
what was built. A twelfth pair is never asked about, and what its value
is made of does not come up:

    RNS/Resource.py#unpack
        adv.t = dictionary["t"]

Reticulum-Go reads the map itself and stops at a value it has no width
for. The vector holds five such pairs, between them every type umsgpack
can write into a packet that this format does not otherwise use: both
signs of fixint, the four signed widths, uint64, both booleans, a
string too long for fixstr, an array and a map at both of the widths a
packet can reach. The eleven fields the advertisement does carry all
print as dashes behind them.

    transfer_size      -        where the corpus holds 1856

rsReticulum and go-reticulum both pass it, and both read the map
through a msgpack library rather than by hand. That is the whole
difference: a library steps over what it does not need, and eleven
names still resolve.

Three vectors added a run later say how wide the gap is, and it is
narrower than the finding was first written. Reticulum-Go passes a
float of four bytes and passes 256 bytes as both a bin16 and a str16,
so its reader does have the widths and does move a length it reads out
of two bytes. What it does not have is the ext family: it fails both
vectors that hold one, and it fails them the same way, with the eleven
fields behind the pair going to dashes. An ext is the one value in
msgpack whose length does not cover the whole of it, because the type
byte sits outside the count, and a reader assembled by hand from the
widths a format uses has no reason to have met one.

cmd/dump had the same defect and the same shape of it, which is the
fifth corpus fault above. It is worth saying twice that two decoders
written independently made it: measuring a value is not the same as
reading it, and only a format with no extension point lets a reader
confuse the two.


Finding 41: a token of 48 bytes is refused at its length before its hmac
is read

Affects Reticulum-rs. Vector test/encrypted/empty-token.

The reference bounds a token twice, and the two bounds are 16 bytes
apart. verify_hmac refuses at 32, because below that there is no hmac
to compare, and everything above it is verified before anything else is
decided:

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#verify_hmac
        if len(token) <= 32: raise

Reticulum-rs bounds it once, at the point where a ciphertext of at
least one block would begin:

    fernet.rs:161   if token_data.len() <= FERNET_OVERHEAD_SIZE
                    that is 48

A token of exactly 48 bytes is an iv and an hmac with nothing between
them, and the hmac over it is right. Reticulum-rs never computes it and
prints

    hmac_valid         no

where the corpus holds yes. The packet is dropped either way and the
two implementations drop it for different reasons, which is the whole
of what this vector separates. The reference drops it one step later,
inside unpad, and that step is finding 37 in a language with no bounds
check.

Reticulum-Go is the only implementation that answers this vector
correctly, and it does it by having the two bounds the reference has.


What the implementations say about themselves

Four of the seven publish a claim about compatibility with python-rns.
Read beside the measurement, the claims are accurate about what they
cover and none of them covers what the corpus asks.

    Reticulum-Go      COMPATIBILITY.md, a table of components against
                      python-rns 1.4.1, and tests/crossref: 48 Go tests
                      against vectors a Python script generates at test
                      time from a checkout of the reference. It is the
                      nearest thing to this corpus that exists in
                      another repository.

    rsReticulum       README, Compatibility Notes: a target version
                      (1.3.8) and a list of utilities not implemented.
                      Tool coverage, not wire coverage.

    Reticulum-rs      tests/python.rs, five integration tests that run
                      a python-rns node beside the Rust one over UDP:
                      announce, link both ways, identify both ways.

    microReticulum    test_interop, four scenarios driven by shell
                      scripts against a Python receiver over UDP, plus
                      one captured advertisement in test/.

    go-reticulum      nine files named *_parity_test.go. They are Go
                      unit tests asserting Go behaviour that was
                      written from reading Python. No Python runs and
                      no reference bytes appear.

    sgiath/reticulum  test/reticulum/reference/, six files that shell
                      out to a Python script per assertion and compare.
                      Closer to a corpus than the name suggests.

    rns.js            none.

Three of these need a running Python and one needs a running Reticulum.
That is the difference the corpus is for: a vector is a file, and a
file can be checked at a commit, on a machine with no Python, in three
and a half seconds, by an implementation that did not exist when the
vector was written.

The two that come closest are worth reading against their results.

Reticulum-Go's crossref generates its vectors from python-rns at the
tag RNS_REF_TAG, which defaults to 1.4.0, and asserts against them in
48 tests. The vectors are not committed, so a run measures whatever
that tag holds today. It scores highest here of the seven by a margin,
and it is the only one whose author can already answer the question
this corpus asks. What it does not have is the rejections: every one of
its 48 tests is an input that decodes, and eight of its 15 failures
here are an input that does not. Six of the eight are a threshold named
at the wrong number and two are a rule it applies and does not report.
That is the half of a wire format a generator writes last, because
nothing in an implementation asks for it. The other seven are findings
28, 32, 35, 39 and 40, the last of them three times, and every one of
them is an input that decodes and a verdict about it that the reference
reaches differently. Finding 29 is a sixteenth failure, on the one kind
it runs that the other six harnesses skip.

sgiath/reticulum has six reference files and a Python runner, and fails
79 vectors here. Its runner covers HKDF, tokens, packet pack and
unpack, IFAC and proofs, all through generated fixtures, and its
identity is loaded from a seed every time. Nothing in it loads an
identity from a stored private key, which is finding 7, and that is the
one call in the library that is broken. A suite passing while the
library cannot do the thing it does on every start after the first is
the case for vectors somebody else wrote.
